    "title": "Capturing Complexity: Studio Victor's Precision Approach",
    "content": "Recording \"And Then You'll Beg\" presented Pierre Rémillard with a problem more demanding than any previous Cryptopsy album: how to capture blast beats running through odd-time signatures with enough definition for each stroke to register individually in the final mix.\n\n**Microphone Setup:**\n- Kick drum: AKG D112 inside the 26-inch shell for attack click, supplemented by a boundary mic at the shell opening for the drum's additional low-frequency body\n- Snare: Shure SM57 top and bottom, with the bottom mic gain reduced to preserve the clean crack of the steel shell\n- Toms: Sennheiser MD421 on each tom, positioned closer than on None So Vile to compensate for the faster fill sequences\n- Hi-hat: AKG C451 with precise isolation to prevent bleed from the larger adjacent cymbal surface\n- Overheads: Two Neumann KM 84s in spaced pair for the Paiste Alpha's brighter overtone profile\n- Room: Minimal — Studio Victor's controlled environment prioritized separation over ambience\n\n**The 26-Inch Challenge:**\nThe larger bass drum presented specific engineering decisions. Its longer resonant decay required more aggressive dampening than smaller kick drums — Rémillard and Flo used a felt muffling strip against the batter head to tighten the attack without completely killing the drum's low-frequency character. The result was a kick sound simultaneously deeper and more controlled than the 22-inch drum of the None So Vile era.\n\n**Odd-Time Documentation:**\nFor the 11/8 and 13/8 blast beat sections, Rémillard used click track references during playback review rather than during tracking — Flo performed these sections without a click, relying on musical feel to maintain the unusual bar lengths. The sessions revealed consistent subdivision accuracy across takes, confirming Flo's internalization of the complex meters.\n\n**Performance Approach:**\nAs on previous albums, Flo tracked complete takes rather than sections, maintaining the performance flow that defines Cryptopsy's recorded energy. The sessions were relatively short by death metal standards — the efficiency of a band that has developed tight internal communication over multiple album cycles.",
